Doug Freyburger wrote:

 > Is there a non-joust melee for the lance?  The times I've tried it
 > has only worked like other poleaxes.  I hit with the hilt if if it
 > were a weak stick.  Or is applying it while not riding not jousting
 > so it's like using a poleam.

You can only joust when you use the lance in melee while riding. You
can also (a)pply it like a regular polearm, while riding or not. This
uses lance skill as well.

 > Because I use corridors a lot to avoid crowds of monsters I seem to
 > use my alternate weapon Excalibur more than the lance.

To use the lance properly, you really need a light source, otherwise
you can't use its full potential in dark areas or corridors.

A typical scenario looks like this: You ride your steed and encounter
a monster. As long as it's in "polearm range", you can apply the lance
to inflict damage from afar. When the monster comes into melee range,
you attack it normally. This can result in a joust (20% at unskilled,
40% at basic, 60% at skilled, 80% at expert). Jousting does 2d10
*additional* damage and robs the monster of all its movement points
for this turn. Monsters of small and normal size are pushed back a
square as well, so you can apply again. See? You take no damage at all
that way! It gets better: Larger monsters aren't pushed back, so you
can joust them again and again and again... until they are dead. If
you position yourself in a way, so that you joust them "against a
wall", this tactic also works for smaller monsters as well. You can
joust arch-liches, minotaurs, etc. this way without any chance for
them to move at all.

Of course, this works best at expert skill where you joust 80% of the
time and can also apply the lance to all squares around you.

